logy. So block is forward chronological. The earlier block (12,13) are chronological forward as well, but appear after the middle block.\n\nOverall, we have three blocks: ending (1,2) reverse? Actually 1 (dies) is final chronologically, 2 (grows old) before death, so order 2 then 1 would be chronological forward; but narrative is 1 then 2 (reverse). So block 1-2 is reverse order relative to chronology.\n\nBlock 3-4 are later events? According to chronology, after 11 (post institute), then 3 (working in hospitals abroad) maybe after war? Could be after? Not sure. But given narrative order 3,4 after 2 (old age) and before wartime block. Hard.\n\nBut the definition of categories: we need best single category. Which fits? Could be ANALEPSIS (dominant timeline with earlier insertions). Dominant timeline perhaps is the wartime block (5-11) which is forward chronological. Inserted scenes 1-4 are later (post-war and old age) and 12-13 are earlier (childhood). So we have both earlier and later insertions. That might be SYLLEPSIS (grouped by principle: the blocks are by period? Blocks: death/old age, later career, wartime, early life). Could be considered SYLLEPSIS because grouping principle is chronological period, not temporal order. But SYLLEPSIS requires no dominant timeline, and scenes form blocks grouped by non-temporal principle (place, character, theme). Here blocks are temporal (period) seems temporal, thus maybe not SYLLEPSIS.\n\nAN ALEPSIS requires dominant timeline >75% scenes. Here 7 out of 13 = ~54%, not >75%. So not fulfill threshold. PROLEPSIS same need >75% later insertions. Not.\n\nZIGZAG requires alternating two strands regularly, not present.\n\nRETROGRADE would be strictly reverse order. Not the case because middle block is forward.\n\nCHRONICLE would be forward order, not.\n\nACHRONY would be cannot assign ranks due to no cues; but we have cues.\n\nThus maybe SYLLEPSIS is best: blocks are grouped by 'life stage' or 'geography'? Not a non-temporal princip